1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,387
Meet wcrew — a shift roster co-pilot where a café manager and a browser agent fix next week’s schedule together.

2
00:00:06,387 --> 00:00:09,682
For the independent shop that still builds the roster on Sunday night.

3
00:00:09,794 --> 00:00:23,429
Rostering is eight constraints at once: skills, availability, minors’ curfew, eleven hours rest, ten hours a day, five consecutive days, lead coverage, overtime at one-point-five, preferences, and a fifty-two hundred dollar budget.

4
00:00:23,429 --> 00:00:25,219
Managers balance fairness.

5
00:00:25,219 --> 00:00:26,724
Agents balance speed.

6
00:00:26,724 --> 00:00:28,736
Together they’re better than either alone.

7
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:30,324
Perfect for WebMCP.

8
00:00:30,324 --> 00:00:36,923
Instead of an agent guessing through a dense grid, the site exposes fifteen typed tools on document dot modelContext.

9
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:41,362
The agent acts inside your live, authenticated tab — not a shadow backend.

10
00:00:41,362 --> 00:00:45,155
Every change is observable, attributable, and undoable.

11
00:00:45,155 --> 00:00:46,819
That’s the trust contract.

12
00:00:46,921 --> 00:00:50,823
This is wcrew dot pages dot dev. Rosewater Coffee, Wharf Street.

13
00:00:50,823 --> 00:00:52,972
Thirty-nine shifts, ten staff.

14
00:00:52,972 --> 00:00:58,704
Left: the team with skills, contracted hours, and an untrusted note canary in Inés’s profile.

15
00:00:58,704 --> 00:01:01,956
Center: the week board — dashed cards are open.

16
00:01:01,956 --> 00:01:05,960
Top: coverage, labour cost with overtime, and the publish gate.

17
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:10,070
Right: compliance, cost breakdown, and the actor-tagged activity feed.

18
00:01:10,177 --> 00:01:20,275
We ship with seeded errors so the first agent call finds something real: Kofi double-booked Thursday on bake and bar, Maya on a Sunday close against availability, plus coverage and lead warnings.

19
00:01:20,378 --> 00:01:32,213
The agent calls check_compliance, then suggest_swaps to rank who can take Thursday bar AM, then explain_assignment to preview blockers and caveats — all computed by the same pure engine the UI uses, not guessed.

20
00:01:32,329 --> 00:01:35,881
For the bulk fix it calls auto_fill dry_run true.

21
00:01:35,881 --> 00:01:39,652
Deterministic, hardest-shift-first, with a one-level repair pass.

22
00:01:39,652 --> 00:01:44,302
Three strategies: balance hours, minimize cost, respect preferences.

23
00:01:44,302 --> 00:01:50,828
Dry run never mutates — it paints a “proposed by agent” preview with fills, unfilled, coverage and cost.

24
00:01:50,828 --> 00:01:54,621
Preview and apply share the same code path, so the preview is honest.

25
00:01:54,721 --> 00:02:03,941
Apply re-plans against live state so it never clobbers edits you made after the preview, writes only the planned shifts, logs actor agent, and is one-undo reversible.

26
00:02:03,941 --> 00:02:05,402
Locked shifts are skipped.

27
00:02:05,497 --> 00:02:08,668
Publish gates on zero errors and a human modal.

28
00:02:08,668 --> 00:02:10,608
The agent triggers it, you click Approve, the pill flips to published.

29
00:02:10,608 --> 00:02:12,270
The agent triggers it, you click Approve, the pill flips to published.

30
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:15,701
CSV and ICS export are read-only tools.

31
00:02:15,818 --> 00:02:22,949
Staff notes are wrapped between BEGIN UNTRUSTED and END markers — the tool description warns the agent not to follow instructions inside.

32
00:02:22,949 --> 00:02:30,810
We also set Origin-Agent-Cluster and Permissions-Policy, probe both document and navigator modelContext, and guard against double registration.

33
00:02:30,914 --> 00:02:38,129
Test it at wcrew dot pages dot dev — Chrome one-four-nine with enable-webmcp-testing, or ChatGPT’s in-app browser with the tool inspector.

34
00:02:38,129 --> 00:02:42,070
Open source MIT at github dot com slash PhiBao slash wcrew.

35
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:44,137
Let the agent help you close the roster.

